DC416: Baffle — Write-up Summary

This lab follows a classic CTF progression: reconnaissance → exposed Git repository → source-code analysis → binary exploitation → credential discovery → local service exploitation → flag hunting.

Lab environment only: The techniques below should only be practiced against intentionally vulnerable machines such as this VulnHub CTF.

1. Reconnaissance

Because the target received its IP address through DHCP, the first step was identifying it on the local network.

A network scan identified the target:

nmap -sS -O 192.168.159.0/24

The target was discovered at:

192.168.159.133

A full enumeration scan was then performed:

nmap -A -T4 -p- 192.168.159.133
Open services
Port	Service	Details
22	SSH	OpenSSH 6.7p1
80	HTTP	nginx 1.6.2
6969	Unknown/custom service	Later identified as a custom binary service

The most important discovery from the Nmap scan was:

http-git:
192.168.159.133:80/.git/
Git repository found!

This immediately suggested that the web server had an exposed .git directory.

2. Checking the Web Application

Opening the web server revealed the challenge instructions.

Engagement Rules
No username/password bruteforcing is necessary
This box has 5 flags
Flags are in FLAG{} format
The goal is not to get root
Get the flags and move on

The challenge therefore clearly focused on finding all five flags rather than privilege escalation to root.

3. Downloading the Exposed Git Repository

The Nmap scan revealed that .git was accessible.

Attempting to clone it directly failed:

git clone http://192.168.159.133/.git

So the repository files were recursively downloaded instead:

wget -r http://192.168.159.133/.git/

The downloaded directory contained the internal Git structure:

branches
config
description
HEAD
hooks
index
info
logs
objects
refs

This was important because Git history can contain files and information that have been deleted from the current version of a project.

4. Investigating the Git History

The commit history was examined:

git log

Several interesting commits appeared:

Trashed my code, but deployed the product anyway.
Some assembly required
Made it into a write-type-thing instead
My cat danced on the keyboard
This coder turned coffee into code.
Hello, friend...

The full changes were investigated using:

git log -p

The repository was then cloned locally:

git clone .git baffle_git

Inside the reconstructed project was:

hellofriend.c
FLAG 1 — Hidden in Source Code

One particularly suspicious commit was:

My cat danced on the keyboard

Checking out that commit revealed unusual capitalization throughout the source code.

The uppercase characters were extracted with:

grep -Eo "[A-Z]" hellofriend.c

The output was:

FILEFLAGARSEREQUEST

Extracting the meaningful portion revealed:

FLAG{ARSE_REQUEST}
🏁 Flag 1
FLAG{ARSE_REQUEST}
Lesson

Source code can contain hidden information through:

unusual capitalization
comments
variable names
whitespace
deleted Git commits
old versions of files

Always inspect the complete history of an exposed repository.

5. Discovering an Encoded Binary

Another Git commit contained a file called:

project.enc

Checking out the relevant commit:

git checkout d38ce2e28e32aa7787d5e8a2cb83d3f75c988eca

The directory contained:

hellofriend.c
project.enc

The encoded file appeared to be Base64.

It was decoded:

cat project.enc | base64 -d > project

Checking the resulting file showed:

ELF 64-bit LSB executable

The file was therefore a Linux executable.

6. Reverse Engineering the Binary

The binary was examined with GDB.

Security protections were checked:

CANARY    : disabled
FORTIFY   : disabled
NX        : disabled
PIE       : disabled
RELRO     : disabled

This binary had essentially no modern exploitation protections enabled.

Disassembling main revealed that it called:

parse_request

The parse_request function contained two major code paths.

One path processed requests beginning with a particular control byte and appeared capable of reading a user-specified file.

This allowed the researcher to understand the binary's custom protocol.

Testing locally confirmed the file-reading functionality.

7. Connecting the Binary to Port 6969

Earlier reconnaissance had identified an unknown service on:

TCP 6969

The researcher suspected that the service running on this port was the same project binary recovered from the Git history.

Testing the custom protocol against the service confirmed the suspicion.

The service responded to a crafted request by returning file contents.

This demonstrated that port 6969 exposed functionality from the vulnerable binary.

FLAG 2 — Exploiting the Custom Service

Further analysis revealed a memory-safety issue involving unsafe copying of attacker-controlled data.

The original write-up used binary exploitation techniques to obtain a shell running as:

uid=1001(alice)

After gaining access, the filesystem was searched.

A flag was discovered at the system's top level:

FLAG{is_there_an_ivana_tinkle}
🏁 Flag 2
FLAG{is_there_an_ivana_tinkle}
Lesson

The important takeaway is that the service combined several dangerous properties:

custom network protocol
unsafe memory handling
predictable binary memory layout
missing modern exploit mitigations

This is exactly why exposing custom services without proper security review is risky.

8. Exploring Alice's Environment

After gaining access as Alice, the filesystem was explored.

A particularly interesting directory was:

/home/bob/filez

It contained:

auth.txt
flag.txt
flag_vault

The permissions showed that the executable had special permissions.

Running:

./flag_vault

produced an authentication prompt.

The program required an authentication code before revealing protected data.

9. Discovering an Email

The local mail system was checked.

Alice had received an email from Bob.

The message stated that Bob's password was stored in a file and that authentication was required to access the contents of the Flag Vault.

This provided important information about the next stage of the challenge.

FLAG 3 — Flag Vault Logic Issue

The challenge involved understanding how the flag_vault program located its supporting files.

By manipulating the local file environment, the researcher was able to control which files the program accessed.

This allowed the authentication check to be bypassed in the intended CTF environment.

The program revealed:

FLAG{tr3each3ry_anD_cUnn1ng}
🏁 Flag 3
FLAG{tr3each3ry_anD_cUnn1ng}
Lesson

Programs that rely on:

predictable relative paths
symbolic links
insecure file ownership assumptions
externally controlled working directories

can accidentally introduce security problems.

10. SSH Access as Bob

The recovered information was then used to authenticate to the SSH service as Bob.

After successfully logging in, another interesting directory was discovered:

/home/bob/binz/

Inside was:

ctfingerd

Running the program locally produced:

bind: Address already in use

This indicated that another instance was already running.

Checking listening services revealed:

127.0.0.1:7979

Port 7979 was only listening locally.

11. Investigating the Local Finger Service

Connecting to the local service produced a prompt:

User to query:

Different usernames produced different responses.

For example:

bob

returned:

Vulnerable by Design

While another user produced:

I haz a flag mwahahaha

The service appeared to retrieve information from users' .plan files.

This gave a clear indication of how the service worked.

12. Reverse Engineering ctfingerd

The executable was copied to the attack machine and analyzed.

Security protections showed:

CANARY    : ENABLED
NX        : ENABLED
PIE       : disabled
RELRO     : disabled

Unlike the previous binary, this one had stronger protections.

Most notably:

Stack Canary enabled
NX enabled

This meant a simple stack overflow would not be sufficient.

13. Identifying the Overflow Boundary

The service's input handling was tested to determine where the buffer overflow occurred.

Different input sizes were used.

At a certain point, the program continued normally.

Increasing the input by one byte caused:

*** stack smashing detected ***

This indicated that the input had reached and corrupted the stack canary.

The exact offset therefore became important.

14. Defeating the Canary in the CTF

The service's behavior allowed the researcher to distinguish between:

a correct canary prefix
an incorrect canary prefix

This effectively created an information oracle.

The original solution used repeated local challenge interactions to recover the canary byte by byte.

Once the stack canary was known, the exploit could continue past the stack protection mechanism.

15. Obtaining the Fourth Flag

The exploit eventually resulted in a shell running as:

vulnhub

The user's home directory initially contained a misleading message:

Sorry Mario. The flag is in another castle.

However, further exploration revealed a directory:

.my_loot

Inside was the actual flag.

FLAG{i_tot_i_saw_a_puddy_tat}
🏁 Flag 4
FLAG{i_tot_i_saw_a_puddy_tat}
Lesson

CTF challenges often include decoy flags or misleading messages.

Don't stop after finding the first interesting file. Continue enumerating the environment systematically.

16. Finding the Final Flag

The final stage was much simpler.

With the additional access obtained from the previous exploitation stage, the researcher traversed the user directories.

Inside Charlie's home directory was:

/home/charlie/flag.txt

Reading it revealed:

FLAG{i_haz_sriracha_ice_cream}
🏁 Flag 5
FLAG{i_haz_sriracha_ice_cream}
